1. DEFINITIONS

 1.1  "Licensor" means franc (Discord: .nlux., ID 1200828694088917114),
      the author and copyright holder of the Software.

 1.2  "Software" means the Vigil Discord bot: its source code, the web
      dashboard, its build and configuration files, and its bundled
      documentation, as published by the Licensor in the original
      repository, including any release or commit thereof.

 1.3  "Modifications" means any changes you make to the Software or to
      a Modified Version — including additions, deletions, patches,
      ports, plugins that link or load against it, and translations.

 1.4  "Modified Version" means the Software with your Modifications
      applied, in source or runnable form.

 1.5  "Notice Files" means the LICENSE file, copyright and attribution
      notices in source headers, the license field of package.json,
      and any "powered by" or authorship notices shipped with the
      Software.

 1.6  "Sell" means to demand payment or other consideration *for the
      Software or a Modified Version itself* — including charging for
      access to it, licensing it onward, sublicensing it, renting it
      out as code, or listing it on any marketplace or code store.
      "Sell" does NOT mean charging for services (see 2.5) and does
      NOT mean a community you run with the Software happens to
      generate revenue (see 5.2).

 1.7  "You" means any individual or entity that receives or uses the
      Software under this license.

2. GRANTS — WHAT YOU MAY DO

Subject to the conditions in section 3 and the restrictions in
section 4, the Licensor grants you a worldwide, royalty-free,
perpetual, non-exclusive license to:

 2.1  USE — Run unlimited instances of the Software on any
      infrastructure you control or rent, for any lawful purpose, on
      any number of Discord servers, whether personal, community, or
      commercially operated.

 2.2  MODIFY — Study, alter, extend, and create Modifications for any
      lawful purpose. You are not required to publish them.

 2.3  DISTRIBUTE — Redistribute the unmodified Software, or your
      Modified Version, to anyone, in source or object form, free of
      charge, provided every condition in section 3 is met.

 2.4  REBRAND DEPLOYMENTS — Configure the Software's user-facing
      branding for your own deployment (dashboard title, bot name,
      avatar, accent colors, footer text). This is use of a product
      feature, not a violation of section 4.2.

 2.5  SELL SERVICES, NOT THE SOFTWARE — You may charge for your time
      and labor around the Software: installation, hosting,
      management, maintenance, support, training, or developing
      custom Modifications for a client. Two conditions:
        (a) the fee is for the service, not for the code; and
        (b) any copy of the Software or Modified Version the client
            receives is delivered under this license at no charge
            for the code itself.

 2.6  CONTRIBUTE — If you submit a pull request, patch, or other
      contribution to the Licensor's repository, you grant the
      Licensor a perpetual, worldwide, royalty-free right to use,
      modify, and redistribute that contribution as part of the
      Software, with no restrictions and no attribution obligation
      beyond this license.

3. CONDITIONS — WHAT EVERY COPY MUST CARRY

 3.1  Keep the Notice Files intact in every copy you distribute,
      and in the source of every Modified Version.

 3.2  If you distribute a Modified Version, it must:
        (a) be licensed to its recipients under this same license
            (Vigil Community Source License 1.0, or a later version
            issued by the Licensor);
        (b) carry a visible notice that it is modified from the
            original Vigil and where the original can be found;
        (c) not charge recipients for the code (section 4.1 applies
            to your recipients through you).

 3.3  Attribution must be human-findable: a link or reference to the
      original project in your fork's README or equivalent landing
      page qualifies; buried minified code does not.

4. RESTRICTIONS — WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

 4.1  NO SELLING THE SOFTWARE. You may not Sell the Software or any
      Modified Version (see 1.6 for exactly what that means). You may
      not bundle the Software or a Modified Version into a paid
      product where the code is what the buyer is paying for.

 4.2  NO PLAGIARISM. You may not claim authorship of the Software,
      present it or any Modified Version as your own original work,
      remove or obscure copyright or attribution notices from the
      source, or misrepresent the origin of the code to anyone.

 4.3  NO TRADEMARK GRAB. The names "Vigil" and "NLux", the Vigil
      logo, and similar marks are reserved for the Licensor and the
      original project. You may not register them, or confusingly
      similar marks, as your own trademark, service mark, or domain.
      Your Modified Version must be named or presented so a reasonable
      person can tell it is a fork of Vigil, not Vigil itself.

 4.4  NO FALSE ENDORSEMENT. You may not imply that the Licensor
      endorses you, your fork, or your services.

 4.5  LAWFUL USE ONLY. You may not use the Software in violation of
      applicable law or Discord's Terms of Service or Community
      Rules.

5. SCOPE CLARIFICATIONS — WHERE THE LINES ACTUALLY ARE

These clauses exist because every license gets the same arguments.
Here are the answers, in advance:

 5.1  Running the bot in a monetized community — a server with paid
      memberships, donations, advertisements, or server boosts — is
      USE under 2.1 and is expressly allowed. The community's revenue
      is not "selling the Software".

 5.2  Accepting donations or tips for your work on a fork is allowed,
      provided the fork's code itself remains available under this
      license at no charge. Donations for the code's development are
      not a fee for the code.

 5.3  Hosting Vigil for clients as a managed service is allowed under
      2.5. You may bill for hosting, uptime, and maintenance. You may
      not bill for the Software itself, and your client's copy falls
      under this license.

 5.4  Publishing forks, mirrors, or archives of the Software is
      allowed under section 3 — the code is meant to circulate. What
      is not allowed is doing so commercially (4.1) or anonymously
      claiming credit (4.2).

 5.5  Using the Software in courses, tutorials, screenshots, videos,
      or write-ups is allowed; attribute the original project where
      reasonably possible.

 5.6  Training machine-learning models on the Software's source is
      permitted only if the resulting model is not used to provide a
      code-generation product trained primarily on this codebase.
      Honest private study and research are always fine.

 5.7  If a use case is not listed anywhere above, apply the principle
      the whole license is built on: the code stays free, the credit
      stays with the author, and nobody sells Vigil — the author
      included. When genuinely unsure, ask (section 10).

